Q1. Linux.?

Answer: Linux is an open-source operating system, that manages CPU, Memory, Disks and Network.

Q2.	How will you troubleshoot network issues in Linux.?

Answer: If there is a network issue, I would check
a. Connectivity Issue with the commands "ping" (ping <ip/host>)
b. DNS using the command "nslookup" (nslookup <domain name>) --> If ping 8.8.8.8 works but nslookup google.com fails, then its a DNS Problem
c. Network Interface is up using the command "ip link show" --> We can use "ifconfig" command also, but it is deprecated.
d. Routing using the command "ip route"
e. If the issue still persist, i will check for the firewall using the command "iptables"
f. If the above mentioned will not sort out the issue, I will go capture the packet using "tcpdump" and use "traceroute" to see where the packets stopped

Q3. LVM in Linux.?

Answer: LVM -> Logical Volume Manager.
It is a storage management system in linux, which gives flexibility to manage the storage.
Basically, disk partitions have a fixed size and are difficult to resize. Here, LVM creates logical volumes, which is easy to increase or decrease whenever we need more or less disk space.

